facts:
Two angles forming a **right angle** add to $90^{\circ}$.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(2.4,0);
\draw (0,0)--(0,2.4);
\draw (0,0)--(40:2.2);
\draw (0.32,0)--(0.32,0.32)--(0,0.32);
\node at (1.17,0.43) {$a$};
\node at (0.53,1.13) {$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Angles on a **straight line** add to $180^{\circ}$.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (-2.5,0)--(2.5,0);
\draw (0,0)--(65:2.1);
\node at (-0.67,1.05) {$a$};
\node at (1.05,0.67) {$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Angles **at a point** (around a vertex) add to $360^{\circ}$.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\draw (0,0)--(0:2.1);
\draw (0,0)--(110:2.1);
\draw (0,0)--(225:2.1);
\node at (0.72,1.02) {$a$};
\node at (-1.22,0.27) {$b$};
\node at (0.48,-1.15) {$c$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Find the missing angle. The square marks a right angle.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0) (0,0)--(0,3) (0,0)--(24:2.8) (0,0)--(55:2.8);
\draw (0.3,0)--(0.3,0.3)--(0,0.3);
\draw (0.9,0) arc (0:24:0.9);
\draw (24:1.2) arc (24:55:1.2);
\draw (55:0.9) arc (55:90:0.9);
\node at (12:1.4) {$24^{\circ}$};
\node at (39.5:1.7) {$31^{\circ}$};
\node at (72.5:1.4) {$x^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Find the missing angle. All three marked angles lie on the same side of the straight line.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\draw (-3,0)--(3,0) (0,0)--(48:2.6) (0,0)--(111:2.6);
\draw (1,0) arc (0:48:1);
\draw (48:1) arc (48:111:1);
\draw (111:1) arc (111:180:1);
\node at (24:1.5) {$48^{\circ}$};
\node at (79.5:1.5) {$x^{\circ}$};
\node at (145.5:1.5) {$69^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Find the missing angle. The four marked sectors fill a complete turn.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\foreach \a in {0,80,175,285}{\draw (0,0)--(\a:2.5);}
\draw (0.9,0) arc (0:80:0.9);
\draw (80:0.9) arc (80:175:0.9);
\draw (175:0.9) arc (175:285:0.9);
\draw (285:0.9) arc (285:360:0.9);
\node at (40:1.5) {$80^{\circ}$};
\node at (127.5:1.5) {$95^{\circ}$};
\node at (230:1.5) {$110^{\circ}$};
\node at (322.5:1.5) {$x^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Find the reflex angle $r^{\circ}$.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\draw (0,0)--(0:2.6) (0,0)--(58:2.6);
\draw (0.8,0) arc (0:58:0.8);
\draw (58:1.15) arc (58:360:1.15);
\node at (29:1.4) {$58^{\circ}$};
\node at (209:1.6) {$r^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

A learner says $a+b=180^{\circ}$ because the marked angles share a vertex. Explain whether this is correct.

